 `yq`, `python` and `node` shadowed by stubs that exit 127. |\n\n## Notes\n\n- S5 is the guard against the scan being a list of keys somebody typed: it re-derives from the\n  document rather than asserting a constant. A constant here would go stale the first time a\n  protocol document gains a key, which is the exact event the scan exists to catch.\n- No `yq`. The workspace's constraint is `bash`, `git`, `protocol` only; top-level keys in these\n  documents are column-zero `key:` lines, which shell can decide.\n- The scan cannot find a closed surface — a closed surface is precisely one with no document key.\n  That limit is stated in the audit by `task:ova-scan-loop`, not here.\n\n## Verifier\n\n`.engineering/checks/check-scan.sh`. S1–S9 are its rows.\n\nS7 copies `protocols/` into `${TMPDIR:-home-path:sha256:22fb0807eac4722ef87189d62f3aa4e588bb714d3a6a49f5886d994c069193f6` and runs the scan against the copy\nvia its root argument; the check fails if the scan cannot be pointed at a root, because a scan that\ncan only read the live tree cannot be tested without mutating it.\n","owner":"protocol","relations":[{"decomposes":"specification:open-vocabulary-audit"},{"derived_from":"task:w4-2-open-vocabulary-audit"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"The derivation: sorted, byte-identical between runs, tree-only, emitting every top-level vocabulary key under protocols/ and every adopter-writable document family under artifacts/.","tags":["adoption","checks"],"title":"scan-declarations.sh: the candidate surfaces the tree declares"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"},"owner":"protocol","relations":[{"decomposes":"specification:open-vocabulary-audit"},{"derived_from":"task:w4-2-open-vocabulary-audit"}],"summary":"The derivation: sorted, byte-identical between runs, tree-only, emitting every top-level vocabulary key under protocols/ and every adopter-writable document family under artifacts/.","tags":["adoption","checks"],"title":"scan-declarations.sh: the candidate surfaces the